## 4. Key Literature Conclusions

1. **Exponential Concentration (Thanasilp et al.)**:
   - Confirmed empirically. The off-diagonal mean in Global Fidelity drops to $0.12 - 0.16$ with vanishing variance, collapsing the Gram matrix toward the identity matrix.
   - Local subsystem tracing replaces the multiplicative factor product with an additive mean, restoring off-diagonal variance ($\sigma^2_{\text{off}} > 0.027$) and preserving discriminative geometric structure.
2. **Spectral Richness (Zendejas-Morales et al.)**:
   - Effective Rank on Global Fidelity is artificially high ($\approx 107 - 142$) due to trivial identity degeneracy (all eigenvalues $\approx 1$).
   - Local kernels compress spectral power into $4 - 9$ dominant principal directions, preventing overfitting. Multiscale kernels provide an optimal, smooth power-law decay with effective ranks of $30 - 50$.
3. **The "Useful" Kernel Threshold (Incudini et al.)**:
   - Avoiding concentration alone is not enough; the kernel must also align with target labels. Local and multiscale kernels consistently achieve higher Centered KTA than global fidelity, which directly translates to improved test accuracy.
